title: d1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Three patterns for how the guide speaks. After a slip, wait, then redirect gently (IDP-01). Ask the owner, alone, before joining or using a starter (IDP-02). Say only what is true, and pass painful truths to a trusted person (IDP-03). Wording and timing are hypotheses until EM-01 Build A.

= Interaction Design Pattern - Wait, then redirect gently [IDP-01] =

**Report Format:** §2.5 Interaction Design Patterns (Interaction Level)

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|IDP-01
|Name|Wait, then redirect gently
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature and group reasoning only. Derived from SA-04, PS-01, PS-02, DS-01, DS-02. Grounded in HFC-01, HFC-03, HFC-04, HFC-06. Not yet evaluated.
|Problem|After a slip, help too soon takes the turn; help too late lets the talk die. A correction or a quiz exposes the slip.
|Principle|The speaker gets the first chance. Then offer both walkers a way back, as a statement.
|Solution|(((
1. Wait: about 8 s after a mid-sentence stop (O-2); keep walking.
1. Place: something here and now. "The pond's just ahead."
1. Thread, to both: "Riet was telling you about pension day."
1. Shared topic, consented this walk: "You both worked for the post."
1. Belief from another time: the feeling, then the present. "You're thinking of your mother. The sun is on the bench." No correction; no confirmation of what is untrue.
1. Word help only if the person's setting allows it, after the wait, as a guess to both: "The sorting office?"
1. Never: "What were you saying?", "You told us that already", "Do you remember...?"
1. Silent again as soon as the talk resumes.
)))
|Research Link|HFC-03: speakers prefer self-repair (Schegloff et al., 1977); repair fails more often in dementia (Orange et al., 1996). HFC-04: common ground. HFC-06: face. HFC-01: the place as a topic that asks nothing of memory.
|Connection to Claims|Shapes F2 (UC01 steps 5, 6; UC02 step 3; UC03 step 2). Rationale for UC01-F2-CL1, UC02-F2-CL2. Tested against UC01-F2-CL3, UC01-F2-CL4.

== Graph edges ==

shapes → F2; supports → UC01-F2-CL1, UC02-F2-CL2; tested-against → UC01-F2-CL3, UC01-F2-CL4; informed-by → EM-01 (Build A).

= Interaction Design Pattern - Ask first [IDP-02] =

**Report Format:** §2.5 Interaction Design Patterns (Interaction Level)

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|IDP-02
|Name|Ask first
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Derived from SA-02, VT-04, VT-07, DS-01. Grounded in HV-01, HV-03; Ryan & Deci (2000); process consent (Dewing, 2007). Not yet evaluated.
|Problem|A personal starter reveals something about its owner. A partner who asks is hard to refuse. Asked in front of the other walker, "no" is hard to say.
|Principle|Ask the owner, alone, before. Two options. "No" is final.
|Solution|(((
1. Ask each walker alone, before the walk: "Riet would like a walk with you. Join her, or not today?"
1. Ask the owner of each starter, alone: "May I mention the post office counter to Jan?"
1. Consent lasts one walk.
1. After a no: no reason asked. The other walker hears only "Not today."
1. Never ask in front of the other walker.
1. No reminders of missed walks; no tallies.
)))
|Research Link|Self-determination theory: choice supports motivation (Ryan & Deci, 2000). Process consent: consent in dementia is renewed at each contact (Dewing, 2007).
|Connection to Claims|Shapes F1. Rationale for UC01-F1-CL6. Tested against UC01-F1-CL8. Verified by UC01-F1-PR1.

== Graph edges ==

shapes → F1; supports → UC01-F1-CL6; tested-against → UC01-F1-CL8; verified-by → UC01-F1-PR1.

= Interaction Design Pattern - Truthful deferral [IDP-03] =

**Report Format:** §2.5 Interaction Design Patterns (Interaction Level)

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|IDP-03
|Name|Truthful deferral
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature and group reasoning only. Derived from SA-05, PS-03, DS-03. Grounded in HV-08, HFC-08. Not yet evaluated.
|Problem|Painful questions come anywhere. A kind lie costs trust. A blunt truth from a stranger, or a machine, causes grief.
|Principle|Say only what is true. Pass painful truths to the trusted person.
|Solution|(((
1. Listed topic (relationship map): "That's one for Myra. Shall I ask her to come?"
1. Alert the trusted person: resident, topic, place.
1. No answer to the question itself; no denial.
1. If the resident declines: the trusted person is told after the walk.
1. The guide does not raise the topic again.
1. Unlisted but possibly painful (a death, an illness, going home): treated as listed; the trusted person is the key worker.
1. The trusted person decides what is said, as agreed with family and ST-06.
)))
|Research Link|HFC-08: lies found out cost trust (Mental Health Foundation, 2016; James et al., 2006).
|Connection to Claims|Shapes F4. Rationale for UC03-F4-CL7. Verified by UC03-F4-PR4.

== Graph edges ==

shapes → F4; supports → UC03-F4-CL7; verified-by → UC03-F4-PR4.

